introduction:
  bigQuestion: "Why is most data useless for AI?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on <strong>Data Fidelity</strong> and <strong>Mathematics</strong>. You will learn that 80% of Machine Learning is just cleaning data (Preprocessing). We will cover how to handle missing values, normalize scales, and perform the foundational math of <strong>Linear Regression</strong> ($y=mx+c$)."
  journey: "You'll start by cleaning a messy dataset (Imputation, Outliers). Then you'll transform it for the machine (One-Hot Encoding, Scaling). Finally, you'll open the 'Black Box' of AI to understand the math behind <strong>Cost Functions</strong> and <strong>Gradient Descent</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Data Cleaning (Garbage In, Garbage Out)"
    icon: "fa-filter"
    content: |
      <p>If you feed an AI bad data, you get bad predictions.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Noise:</strong> Random errors or irrelevant data.</li>
          <li><strong>Imputation:</strong> filling in missing values (e.g., using the average).</li>
          <li><strong>Outliers:</strong> Extreme values that warp the model (e.g., a house costing $1 Billion).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Feature Engineering"
    icon: "fa-sliders"
    content: |
      <p>Making data easier for the machine to understand.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>One-Hot Encoding:</strong> Converting categories (Red, Blue) into numbers (0, 1).</li>
          <li><strong>Normalization:</strong> Squishing data into a range (0 to 1) so large numbers don't dominate.</li>
          <li><strong>Dimensionality Reduction:</strong> Removing useless columns to speed up learning.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Linear Regression (The Math)"
    icon: "fa-chart-line"
    content: |
      <p>The simplest form of Machine Learning.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Formula:</strong> $y = mx + c$ (Line of Best Fit).</li>
          <li><strong>Cost Function (MSE):</strong> A way to calculate "How wrong is the line?"</li>
          <li><strong>Gradient Descent:</strong> The algorithm that nudges the line until errors are minimized.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Preprocessing"
    definition: "The process of preparing raw data for a machine learning model (cleaning, formatting)."
  - term: "Imputation"
    definition: "Replacing missing data with substituted values (e.g., mean, median)."
  - term: "Outlier"
    definition: "A data point that differs significantly from other observations."
  - term: "One-Hot Encoding"
    definition: "Converting categorical data variables into a form that could be provided to ML algorithms (Binary columns)."
  - term: "Normalization"
    definition: "Rescaling numeric attributes to a common range, usually 0 to 1."
  - term: "Linear Regression"
    definition: "An algorithm that models the relationship between two variables by fitting a linear equation."
  - term: "Mean Squared Error (MSE)"
    definition: "A cost function that measures the average of the squares of the errors (difference between predicted and actual)."
  - term: "Gradient Descent"
    definition: "An optimization algorithm used to minimize the cost function by iteratively moving towards the steepest descent."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "More Data is Better"
    correction: "Not if it's noisy! 1000 clean rows are better than 1,000,000 noisy ones. 'Quality over Quantity'."
  - mistake: "The Computer 'Understands' Categories"
    correction: "Computers only know numbers. You MUST convert 'Cat/Dog' to '0/1' (Encoding) or the math breaks."
  - mistake: "Gradient Descent finds Global Minimum"
    correction: "Sometimes it gets stuck in a 'Local Minimum' (a small valley, not the bottom of the ocean)."

